      "id": "moiad8v",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "1. WHAT IS MATTER?"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Matter is made up of tiny particles that have space between them, attract each other, and are continuously moving.",
        "The three basic states of matter are solid, liquid, and gas, distinguished by particle arrangement and kinetic energy.",
        "Evaporation is a surface phenomenon occurring at any temperature below the boiling point and always causes a cooling effect.",
        "Sublimation is the direct change of state from solid to gas without changing into the liquid state.",
        "Latent heat of fusion is the heat energy required to convert 1 kg of solid into liquid at atmospheric pressure at its melting point."
      ],
      "id": "l50gdsh"
    },
